      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-17533",
      ":question/problem": "Given the function $f(x,y; a,b) = a - bx^{2}+y^3$, compute $\\nabla_{a,b} \\: f.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To compute $\\nabla_{a,b} \\: f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $a$ and $b$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  a}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  a}} ( a - b {x}^{2} + {y}^{3} ) = 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  b}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  b}} ( a - b {x}^{2} + {y}^{3} ) = - {x}^{2}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\mathrm{\\nabla }}_{a , b} \\hspace{0.222em} f & \\displaystyle  = \\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  a}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  b}}\\end{array} \\right] \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle - {x}^{2}\\end{array} \\right]\\end{array}\n$$"
    },
